{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on exact head 2a273cf8baabe5dcef0eec82cc6cf4f9a90ebc84. Architecture: this correctly centralizes layer 7 resident browser headers and documents the contract, with outer composition covering trusted-host and deadline responses; however the claimed endpoint/UI contract is not met because the shipped React source has active inline style properties in RebuildNotice, Thread, RecordDetail, and Avatar. Security: frame-ancestors 'none' plus X-Frame-Options DENY is correct, as are default-src 'none', same-origin executable/fetch sources, base-uri/form-action/object-src 'none', nosniff, and no-referrer. But style-src 'self' does not permit inline style attributes. CSP Level 3 applies style-src/style-src-attr checks to inline styles, so the six React style sites can be blocked. TestEmbeddedUILoadsUnderTheBrowserPolicy does not run a browser and explicitly overlooks React's style application; scanning for cssText/insertRule cannot prove the rendered UI obeys CSP. Repair by removing or safely redesigning those inline-style sites, or by explicitly narrowing and documenting the style attribute policy, and add a browser-level assertion that the shipped page renders and reports no CSP violations. Simplification: BrowserHeaders is small and the two placements serve distinct compositions, but the bundle-regex test is complex and gives false confidence; replace that inference with the direct browser contract test rather than extending the regex.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2361b61fbaf705b0489ce25d54822f3e324d60d3","head":"2a273cf8baabe5dcef0eec82cc6cf4f9a90ebc84","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:2f6cc3928d93eb48e1549cd79353ec02be2890cd\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:ab02f3738a43b1c8367ba37e9770b6ec2fe82e90","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}